{
 "title": "The Doctor Who Said Wash Your Hands",
 "facts": "Ignaz Semmelweis, Vienna General Hospital. Two maternity clinics: the First (doctors and students) had about 10% of mothers die of childbed fever in the 1840s; the Second (midwives) had less than half that. Women begged not to be admitted to the First Clinic. In 1847 his colleague Jakob Kolletschka was cut by a student's scalpel during an autopsy and died of a similar illness. Doctors went from autopsies to deliveries without washing. From May 1847 Semmelweis made staff wash in chlorinated lime; the First Clinic's death rate fell to about 1-2% (1.27% in 1848). Many colleagues rejected and ridiculed the idea; his appointment was not renewed in 1849 and he left Vienna. Germ theory (Pasteur, Lister) explained it in the 1860s. Semmelweis noticed that women who gave birth on the way to the hospital ('street births') rarely got the fever.",
